Tooth and Nail Records may
still be a thing but that was a.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:38.320
They had like alternative bands.

00:45:38.320 --> 00:45:39.470
MxPx.

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:42.410
That were pretty, they
were more legitimate.

00:45:42.410 --> 00:45:45.930
MxPx was a punk band
that was kind of like.

00:45:45.930 --> 00:45:47.637
Are they Christian are they not?

00:45:47.637 --> 00:45:52.190
Well, NOFX was a very well known punk band

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:54.490
that then, okay, so then it's okay,

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:56.410
let's do our Christian version of that,

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:57.985
MxPx instead of NOFX.

00:45:57.985 --> 00:46:01.863
But also, we also had a little bit of.

00:46:01.863 --> 00:46:03.030
You had a lip ring.

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:06.670
A judgemental eye towards
Tooth and Nail bands

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:10.010
because the way that we
would do our music is,

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:11.330
it was pretty explicit,

00:46:11.330 --> 00:46:14.270
I mean, musically, it
was all over the place,

00:46:14.270 --> 00:46:17.300
lyrically, it was also all over the place

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.450
but it was pretty explicitly christian

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:22.180
and then we would do
invitations at our concerts

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:24.210
just to, I know we
talked about this before

00:46:24.210 --> 00:46:25.623
but just to remind you,

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:29.250
that's when at the end of your concert,

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:31.230
you start getting very serious

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:33.420
and the music gets very serious

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:36.890
and then Link in this case,
who was the lead singer,

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:41.890
would begin to and just, you know this man

00:46:41.990 --> 00:46:45.740
and just think about giving
17 year old Link Neal,

00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:47.740
the one that was throwing
gum in his girlfriend's hair,

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:51.850
the responsibility of
speaking off the cuff

00:46:51.850 --> 00:46:54.730
about Jesus and God and
the plan of salvation

00:46:54.730 --> 00:46:56.920
and then offering an opportunity

00:46:56.920 --> 00:46:59.930
for people to make a decision
for Christ in the moment,

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:02.740
I so wish we had videotape of this

00:47:05.350 --> 00:47:07.810
but that's how we did our concerts.

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:10.780
So I don't know why we
looked down our nose at MxPx,

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:13.790
because they were legitimately talented.

00:47:13.790 --> 00:47:16.190
We looked down our nose
at them, not musically,

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:19.430
we looked down our nose
at them a little bit in

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:21.187
or maybe side eye to be like,

00:47:21.187 --> 00:47:22.990
are these guys legit Christians

00:47:22.990 --> 00:47:24.290
or are they just kind of like.

00:47:24.290 --> 00:47:25.330
California Christians.

00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:28.180
Yeah, I mean, how
Christian are these guys?

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:29.570
So what do you remember about the show?

00:47:29.570 --> 00:47:32.170
I remember going to the MxPx show

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:34.630
and there was other
bands that were playing,

00:47:34.630 --> 00:47:36.950
surprisingly, not a lot of people there

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:41.200
and then I just remember
going to the bathroom.

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:42.060
Yeah, that's what I remember.

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:46.120
And the lead singer was
pissing at a urinal.

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:46.953
And so were we.

00:47:46.953 --> 00:47:48.909
And so we did too.

00:47:48.909 --> 00:47:50.270
Do you remember saying something to him

00:47:50.270 --> 00:47:51.320
while he was pissing?

00:47:52.539 --> 00:47:54.790
'Cause I don't think I would've done that,

00:47:54.790 --> 00:47:56.823
I've had people do that
to me many times but.

00:47:56.823 --> 00:47:58.940
I probably did, yeah,

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:00.340
I was like, I don't know what I said.

00:48:00.340 --> 00:48:03.127
Hey man, love your music.

00:48:03.127 --> 00:48:04.700
Something nice.

00:48:04.700 --> 00:48:05.533
How's your wiener?

00:48:05.533 --> 00:48:06.533
Something.

00:48:07.595 --> 00:48:08.720
Shaking it.

00:48:08.720 --> 00:48:10.143
An insincere compliment.

00:48:12.410 --> 00:48:15.400
That's all I remember about that,

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:16.960
the Tooth and Nail of it all,

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:20.820
I do remember going to Carolyns
for the christian music day

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:25.750
and Audio Adrenaline was
headlining that festival

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:30.750
and I remember being really
into the music that day,

00:48:31.730 --> 00:48:34.460
'cause you're kinda in this
youth group setting, but boy.

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:36.690
Yeah, they were good, it
was pouring down rain.

00:48:36.690 --> 00:48:38.440
No but I have but it was covered,

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:41.560
the venue at Carolyns was largely covered

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:43.080
so we got under that partly.

00:48:43.080 --> 00:48:45.150
I remember already being soaked.

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:48.120
But don't you remember
that song, Big Big House?

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:50.110
Yeah, with lots and lots of room,

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:52.290
a big, big table with lots of food,

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:55.110
a big, big yard where
we can play football,

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:57.483
a big, big house, it's my father's house.

00:48:57.483 --> 00:48:59.410
This a song about heaven

00:48:59.410 --> 00:49:01.140
that seems like it's for preschoolers.

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:02.290
But here's the thing,

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:06.180
I feel like I can stand
up for them a little bit

00:49:06.180 --> 00:49:08.230
because if you go back
and listen to this song,

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:10.580
it is like the cheesiest
song you can imagine

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:13.170
and you can't imagine any
teenagers ever being into it,

00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:17.080
however, so much of nineties
music was like that,

00:49:17.080 --> 00:49:17.913
even the non-Christian stuff.

00:49:17.913 --> 00:49:21.290
I mean, musically, they had
chops as far as I remember.

00:49:21.290 --> 00:49:24.040
The lyrics in nineties music

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:28.570
with the very few exceptions
was just so, like just,

00:49:28.570 --> 00:49:29.972
I wrote this on the back of
a napkin three seconds ago.

00:49:29.972 --> 00:49:33.280
But we didn't listen to christian music,

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:36.760
we listened to secular
music, we listened to 311,

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:39.850
we tried to, we're gonna emulate them

00:49:39.850 --> 00:49:42.190
but we're gonna do this legitimately

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:44.200
in a Christian band circle

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:49.140
but I remember going to
freshman year in college,

00:49:49.140 --> 00:49:53.670
Incubus came, they were
releasing their second album

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:56.570
and I had discovered them the year before

00:49:56.570 --> 00:49:59.300
and we started emulating them in the band.

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:00.210
And this was kind of weird

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:04.370
because we were sort of
peaking in our Christian,

00:50:04.370 --> 00:50:06.990
not peaking but we were
getting to a very high level

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:09.570
of sincere Christian commitment,

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.350
in that every area of our lives

00:50:11.350 --> 00:50:14.670
was affected by our sort
of commitment to Jesus.

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:18.230
Oh yeah, so but I was
a huge fan of Incubus,

00:50:18.230 --> 00:50:19.063
I knew that the.

00:50:19.063 --> 00:50:20.680
Which literally means the demon that comes

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:22.540
and screws you when you're sleeping.

00:50:22.540 --> 00:50:26.440
Yeah but the, I knew that the man,

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:28.780
this picture of the guy from
the seventies on the cover

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:33.330
of their albums was the
lead singer, Brandon's dad,

00:50:33.330 --> 00:50:35.720
I had done my internet research,

00:50:35.720 --> 00:50:37.070
that's the only reason
I used the internet,

00:50:37.070 --> 00:50:39.698
was to look at band's
websites, I was like, man.

00:50:39.698 --> 00:50:40.580
Band's dads.

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:43.970
They're coming to Mission
Valley, really small venue

00:50:43.970 --> 00:50:46.943
and then I went there,
we watched the show,

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:50.470
this, Brandon still
had dreads at the time,

00:50:50.470 --> 00:50:52.900
you went to the show with
me but then I was like,

00:50:52.900 --> 00:50:55.460
I'm gonna hang out, I wanna meet him,

00:50:55.460 --> 00:51:00.460
I wanna talk to him and
so I waited a while.

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:01.543
I remember this.

00:51:01.543 --> 00:51:04.250
I waited for a long time and then,

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:07.200
actually, maybe I went early

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:08.560
'cause I remember it being daylight,

00:51:08.560 --> 00:51:10.530
it had to have been daylight,
it had to have been early,

00:51:10.530 --> 00:51:12.950
so I go early and I'm hanging around

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:16.260
and then I see him
walking around and he's,

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:19.630
there's a laundromat on
the other side of the venue

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:22.170
and he's going to do his
laundry from the tour bus

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:25.660
and I meet Brandon and
I start talking to him

00:51:25.660 --> 00:51:28.090
and I talk to him for a long time

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:33.090
and my goal was to talk
about Jesus with him.

00:51:35.562 --> 00:51:40.010
I wanted to save the lead
singer of incubus' soul,

00:51:40.010 --> 00:51:43.233
because I was such a huge
fan, how wholesome is that?

00:51:43.233 --> 00:51:46.020
Does that make up for me being an asshole?

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:48.070
Actually, that is sort of
very assholish in on itself.

00:51:48.070 --> 00:51:51.250
Yeah, in a way it is but
I cared about him so much

00:51:51.250 --> 00:51:52.430
and I liked him so much

00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:56.910
but I wanted to, I knew that the only way

00:51:56.910 --> 00:51:58.790
I could connect with him is
if he thought I was cool,

00:51:58.790 --> 00:52:02.340
so I didn't ever really talk about Jesus.

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:05.260
I got this friend, man, he's
got long hair and a beard.

00:52:05.260 --> 00:52:06.950
I asked him about his spiritual beliefs

00:52:06.950 --> 00:52:07.783
and he was very nice.

00:52:07.783 --> 00:52:08.830
He's kind of a hippy.

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:13.250
And he talked to me for, it
could have been an hour, man.

00:52:13.250 --> 00:52:14.712
I remember you telling me
about this conversation

00:52:14.712 --> 00:52:16.890
'cause I was not present
for, I went to the concert

00:52:16.890 --> 00:52:19.450
but I didn't go to the conversation.

00:52:19.450 --> 00:52:20.743
It was awkward, man, it was awkward.

00:52:20.743 --> 00:52:22.460
It probably would've been
shorter if I'd have been there,

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:23.943
hey, we got somewhere to be.

00:52:24.820 --> 00:52:27.760
I think I got his email address.

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:30.968
You still got the lead singer
of Incubus' email address?

00:52:30.968 --> 00:52:31.801
Yeah, maybe.

00:52:31.801 --> 00:52:32.634
You should use it.

00:52:32.634 --> 00:52:34.413
I mean, I need to dig
up my Hotmail account.

00:52:34.413 --> 00:52:36.430
Are they still an active band?

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:39.160
Yeah, I mean they got
much bigger after that

00:52:39.160 --> 00:52:40.570
with their third or fourth album.

00:52:40.570 --> 00:52:43.140
Well, speaking of band's dads,

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:48.140
just a little sidebar here,
Post Malone's dad tweeted,

00:52:52.900 --> 00:52:55.870
he was like, here's a little
something to brighten your day

00:52:55.870 --> 00:52:57.818
and it was our GMM episode.

00:52:57.818 --> 00:52:58.980
Oh really?

00:52:58.980 --> 00:53:00.090
Yeah, so he's like.

00:53:00.090 --> 00:53:00.923
That's hilarious.

00:53:00.923 --> 00:53:02.010
I just love the fact that his.

00:53:02.010 --> 00:53:03.139
His dad tweets.

00:53:03.139 --> 00:53:06.100
Yeah, he doesn't have that many followers

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:08.427
but he's very proud of his boy and.

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:11.200
I gotta look at that.

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:15.740
But I was thinking about going even,

00:53:15.740 --> 00:53:19.313
well, the first concert
I remember is with,

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:22.600
my dad taking me to Beach
Boys at Walnut Creek,

00:53:22.600 --> 00:53:25.510
so Walnut Creek ended up being kind of,

00:53:25.510 --> 00:53:27.640
I think it kinda changed
our lives in a lot of ways,

00:53:27.640 --> 00:53:28.500
'cause they built Walnut Creek.

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:30.575
New venue, outdoor amphitheater
on the edge of Raleigh.

00:53:30.575 --> 00:53:32.890
And now all these big bands

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:34.430
had an excuse to come to Raleigh

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:36.330
in a way that maybe they
weren't coming before,

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:37.320
I don't know, maybe they were just going

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:38.870
to Dorton Arena in Fayetteville,

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:41.370
there wasn't a place
for people to play music

00:53:41.370 --> 00:53:42.480
that I remember.

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:45.130
Unless it was like Carter
Finley stadium, like huge

00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:48.650
but yeah, Dorton Arena
back in the seventies

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:52.040
and it's in Raleigh had like Led Zeppelin.

00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:54.160
You know who else has played Dorton Arena?

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:54.993
We have.

00:53:54.993 --> 00:53:55.826
Red Link.

00:53:55.826 --> 00:53:57.793
And Merle Haggard who we saw there twice.

00:53:59.500 --> 00:54:02.010
The Beach Boys were playing and of course.

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:03.130
You were young.

00:54:03.130 --> 00:54:06.630
I was in seventh or eighth
grade and went with my dad

00:54:07.700 --> 00:54:11.210
and John Stamos was the drummer,

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:12.943
I don't know if you remember.

00:54:12.943 --> 00:54:13.776
Don't know Jesse.

00:54:13.776 --> 00:54:16.930
I don't know how long he did that gig

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:19.612
but he would drum for the beach boys,

00:54:19.612 --> 00:54:21.340
it was kinda like John Mayer playing,

00:54:21.340 --> 00:54:22.580
being the lead of Dead and Co

00:54:22.580 --> 00:54:27.280
but not really anything
close to that but so.

00:54:27.280 --> 00:54:28.750
Did, you thought it was awesome though.

00:54:28.750 --> 00:54:30.283
I thought, of course.

00:54:30.283 --> 00:54:31.480
This is Kokomo days.

00:54:31.480 --> 00:54:33.563
'Cause Full House was still happening.

00:54:34.660 --> 00:54:35.493
No, it wasn't.

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:39.610
In seven, in 19, okay, when did it,

00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:41.446
Full House had just gone
off the air in 1991, 1990.

00:54:41.446 --> 00:54:44.210
I don't know, maybe you're right.

00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:46.210
1990, I don't know, man, it might.

00:54:46.210 --> 00:54:47.330
Did they play Kokomo?

00:54:47.330 --> 00:54:48.200
Of course they did.

00:54:48.200 --> 00:54:50.180
That was, I mean that was a big thing.

00:54:50.180 --> 00:54:53.800
I just remember, so the cool
thing about Walnut Creek

00:54:53.800 --> 00:54:57.660
is the lawn, I never, I
actually don't remember

00:54:57.660 --> 00:54:59.170
ever sitting in the seats,

00:54:59.170 --> 00:55:01.213
it was always the lawn seats, lawn.

00:55:01.213 --> 00:55:03.720
'Cause you could get a
ticket for like 30 dollars.

00:55:03.720 --> 00:55:05.830
Yeah and my dad and I are in the lawn

00:55:05.830 --> 00:55:09.950
and there are these
two increasingly drunk,

00:55:09.950 --> 00:55:12.340
middle aged ladies next to us

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:15.610
and then I don't remember
exactly how it happened

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:18.440
but my dad and I just end up
slow dancing with both of them.

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:21.370
What, are you serious?

00:55:21.370 --> 00:55:25.430
Yeah not, just sort of just
like having a good time

00:55:26.680 --> 00:55:28.780
and not for that many songs

00:55:28.780 --> 00:55:31.142
but it just was something
that just happened

00:55:31.142 --> 00:55:32.120
and it was, they were.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:35.130
Did you and you never spoke
of it again, kinda a thing.

00:55:36.002 --> 00:55:37.409
It was just like, hey, we're just kinda.

00:55:37.409 --> 00:55:38.690
Did Diane hear about this?

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:39.787
Of course she did, it wasn't like,

00:55:39.787 --> 00:55:42.350
so it wasn't a scandalous thing,

00:55:42.350 --> 00:55:45.030
it was just a kind of a fun thing.

00:55:45.030 --> 00:55:46.822
It was kind of like the
community square dance

00:55:46.822 --> 00:55:48.508
where everybody can dance together

00:55:48.508 --> 00:55:49.797
and you're gonna go back,

00:55:49.797 --> 00:55:52.207
you're gonna ride your own
saddle back home kind of thing.

00:55:52.207 --> 00:55:54.140
And it didn't get,

00:55:54.140 --> 00:55:58.130
it never turned sexual even on any level,

00:55:58.130 --> 00:56:00.163
it was just very much just
like, this is a bit crazy.

00:56:00.163 --> 00:56:03.749
But I know how you thought as
a seventh and eighth grader.

00:56:03.749 --> 00:56:05.193
Well, what do you mean?

00:56:06.270 --> 00:56:08.000
Yeah but I wasn't thinking
about middle aged ladies

00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:08.870
at that point.

00:56:08.870 --> 00:56:09.900
You weren't?

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.350
I'm talking, these ladies
were in their fifties.

00:56:11.350 --> 00:56:14.610
Oh, okay, alright, I remember the.

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:15.457
There's nothing wrong with that now,

00:56:15.457 --> 00:56:16.653
I'm 44 but I'm just saying when I was 13.

00:56:18.698 --> 00:56:21.647
The first concert I remember going to was,

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:26.901
my mom took me and you to see.

00:56:26.901 --> 00:56:28.320
MC Hammer.
MC Hammer.

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:31.160
At the Dean Smith center, yeah, man

00:56:31.160 --> 00:56:33.750
and that was huge, I was such a,

00:56:33.750 --> 00:56:37.080
I mean, this is like, can't touch this,

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:41.750
so he's such a huge moment in pop music.

00:56:41.750 --> 00:56:43.840
And you know, I was huge MC Hammer fan,

00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:45.110
you were, you liked him

00:56:45.110 --> 00:56:48.920
but I had completely drunk the Kool-Aid.

00:56:48.920 --> 00:56:50.300
Something about yeah, 'cause.

00:56:50.300 --> 00:56:52.653
I didn't understand how much of a,

00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:55.640
how not good his music was

00:56:55.640 --> 00:56:58.030
compared to all the rest of the
hip hop that was being made.

00:56:58.030 --> 00:56:58.863
Oh yeah, because we were.

00:56:58.863 --> 00:57:00.740
I was just a little Southern white boy

00:57:00.740 --> 00:57:01.870
that didn't really understand it.

00:57:01.870 --> 00:57:04.540
Yeah, we were 13 years old

00:57:06.253 --> 00:57:09.980
and it, I mean, everybody loved MC Hammer,

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:13.480
he was a pop icon, he
wasn't a hip hop icon.

00:57:13.480 --> 00:57:14.770
Yeah, it was a different thing.

00:57:14.770 --> 00:57:16.820
And it, the hiphop community was going,

00:57:16.820 --> 00:57:17.910
was coming after him.

00:57:17.910 --> 00:57:20.510
They were rapping about
him and making fun of him

00:57:20.510 --> 00:57:22.070
and I was like, why are
they after MC Hammer?

00:57:22.070 --> 00:57:23.700
Well just another point of reference

00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:26.260
to kind of bring this
into complete clarity,

00:57:26.260 --> 00:57:29.040
Vanilla Ice was touring with him,

00:57:29.040 --> 00:57:32.330
so, I mean, there weren't
many hip hop artists

00:57:32.330 --> 00:57:34.557
who were gonna let
Vanilla Ice open for them.

00:57:34.557 --> 00:57:35.630
And En Vogue.

00:57:35.630 --> 00:57:37.510
Vanilla Ice did not show up that night,

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:41.511
we found out when we got
there and I was so upset.

00:57:41.511 --> 00:57:44.040
Well, I think we were more
excited about Vanilla Ice,

00:57:44.040 --> 00:57:46.656
to be honest with you, at that time.

00:57:46.656 --> 00:57:48.906
I mean, just think about
that, it's En Vogue,

00:57:50.430 --> 00:57:52.087
Vanilla Ice and MC hammer.

00:57:52.087 --> 00:57:54.713
It's a big night and my mom.

00:57:54.713 --> 00:57:56.410
And your mom was there, Sue was there.

00:57:56.410 --> 00:58:01.110
My mom and my mom was into
it, she was loving it.

00:58:01.110 --> 00:58:02.410
But Vanilla Ice didn't show up

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:04.690
but En Vogue really brought it.

00:58:04.690 --> 00:58:07.400
What a man, what a man,
what a mighty good man.

00:58:07.400 --> 00:58:10.490
I remember feeling things.

00:58:10.490 --> 00:58:14.160
I don't remember a lot about
the show but it was huge,

00:58:14.160 --> 00:58:15.930
it was like a big stage production,

00:58:15.930 --> 00:58:17.580
it was like the biggest
thing I've ever seen.

00:58:17.580 --> 00:58:19.940
Many years later, you and I went and saw

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:22.550
the Stone Temple Pilots
at the Dean Smith Center

00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:28.620
as sophomores in college
and that was a, that show,

00:58:28.620 --> 00:58:30.460
I remember that show sounding really good.

00:58:30.460 --> 00:58:32.427
Yeah, that music sounded
great in that venue.

00:58:32.427 --> 00:58:37.427
For it to be four guys just playing,

00:58:37.490 --> 00:58:39.688
I mean they were, it
was really impressive.

00:58:39.688 --> 00:58:40.521
And not just that,

00:58:40.521 --> 00:58:43.250
four guys and one of them is
just singing, I mean, the.

00:58:44.099 --> 00:58:45.010
One guitarist.

00:58:45.010 --> 00:58:49.890
The guitarist in that band
doing a show like that,

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:52.023
yeah, it's crazy impressive.

00:58:53.580 --> 00:58:55.590
The guitarist and bassist were brothers,

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:56.980
the DeLeo brothers or something,

00:58:56.980 --> 00:59:00.360
so they were, yeah, they brought it, man,

00:59:00.360 --> 00:59:02.730
I was, that's the first
time I went to a show

00:59:02.730 --> 00:59:06.380
and I was paying attention to the sound,

00:59:06.380 --> 00:59:08.390
this actually sounded good

00:59:08.390 --> 00:59:09.953
and I remember our
friend Josh wanted to go

00:59:09.953 --> 00:59:12.090
but for some reason we didn't invite him,

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:15.610
I don't know, sorry Josh,
still a little bit of an.

00:59:15.610 --> 00:59:18.483
Was it the Brooks and Dunn
concert at Walnut Creek?

00:59:20.270 --> 00:59:23.170
So, we saw a number of
acts at Walnut Creek,

00:59:23.170 --> 00:59:24.170
including Van Halen,

00:59:24.170 --> 00:59:25.003
which we weren't even
fans of, just because.

00:59:25.003 --> 00:59:28.160
That wasn't our idea, we
saw George Strait there

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:31.980
and we all got in the, Joey
Smith drove his dad's suburban

00:59:31.980 --> 00:59:35.290
and it was like nine of us in the suburban

00:59:35.290 --> 00:59:37.230
and we were in the very back

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:39.520
and we were going to George Strait,

00:59:39.520 --> 00:59:41.720
so we were cowboyed up,

00:59:41.720 --> 00:59:43.910
we might have been wearing cowboy hats,

00:59:43.910 --> 00:59:46.010
definitely cowboy shirts

00:59:46.010 --> 00:59:49.907
and we were doing dip in the back.

00:59:49.907 --> 00:59:51.027
In the back of a suburban,
not a great idea.

00:59:51.027 --> 00:59:54.270
And I got so sick.

00:59:54.270 --> 00:59:56.120
You gotta wait till you
get to the concert to dip

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:58.020
but was that the, which concert was it

00:59:58.020 --> 01:00:00.630
that somebody had tequila?

01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:02.330
That was it, it was Brooks and Dunn,

01:00:02.330 --> 01:00:05.910
so there was this huge tailgating thing

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:07.740
happening in the parking lot

01:00:07.740 --> 01:00:11.567
and I, we were just 16, 17 years old

01:00:13.890 --> 01:00:16.900
and I'd never, we'd never been to parties

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:21.540
where people are drinking
liquor and just raising hell

01:00:21.540 --> 01:00:22.740
and it's like, so we're walking around

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:23.900
and I'm talking to people,

01:00:23.900 --> 01:00:27.720
I'm like, these rednecks at their tailgate

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:29.740
turning up tequila bottles and I'm like,

01:00:29.740 --> 01:00:32.360
what's up guys, Brooks and Dunn, woo.

01:00:32.360 --> 01:00:34.700
It's like, this is my, I'm like, woo,

01:00:34.700 --> 01:00:36.280
you would think that I was drunk

01:00:36.280 --> 01:00:38.040
when I hadn't had a drink

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:42.540
and then once you start being
friendly with these people,

01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:45.134
they're like, hey man, you want some,

01:00:45.134 --> 01:00:47.153
you want a shot of tequila?

01:00:47.153 --> 01:00:49.140
And I'm like, oh, you called my bluff,

01:00:49.140 --> 01:00:54.010
I'm like, nah and then I
don't remember how it happened

01:00:54.010 --> 01:00:56.453
but it was like but I'll eat the worm,

01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:59.190
I think that was my response, it was like.

01:00:59.190 --> 01:01:00.650
I'll eat a worm though.

01:01:00.650 --> 01:01:01.540
I'm not gonna.

01:01:01.540 --> 01:01:02.547
I'm not approved.

01:01:02.547 --> 01:01:04.510
You're calling my bluff,

01:01:04.510 --> 01:01:06.520
I'm not actually going to drink

01:01:06.520 --> 01:01:08.380
but I will eat the worm 'cause

01:01:09.407 --> 01:01:11.660
and he was like, all right and he got the.

01:01:11.660 --> 01:01:12.520
Get that boy the worm.

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:15.300
He got the worm out of the
bottom of the tequila bottle

01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:17.720
and I ate it.

01:01:17.720 --> 01:01:19.670
Wasn't the first time
you've eaten the worms anus.

01:01:19.670 --> 01:01:20.850
Yeah, it was the second

01:01:20.850 --> 01:01:23.370
and it definitely wouldn't be the last,

01:01:23.370 --> 01:01:24.940
who knew that I'd make an
entire career out of it?

01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:26.710
You didn't eat the worm.

01:01:26.710 --> 01:01:28.090
No, I actually don't believe

01:01:28.090 --> 01:01:30.170
that I was present for that moment

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:33.030
because I remember hearing about it

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:34.583
and feeling uneasy about it,

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:36.900
'cause that was in that moment

01:01:36.900 --> 01:01:38.820
where our friends were
beginning to kind of party

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:40.810
and we were still a member of the group

01:01:40.810 --> 01:01:43.380
and kinda like what you're saying,

01:01:43.380 --> 01:01:45.920
we never had prudish personalities,

01:01:45.920 --> 01:01:48.530
like if you talked to us
and you hung out with us,

01:01:48.530 --> 01:01:51.470
you wouldn't be like, these
guys are super straight edge.

01:01:51.470 --> 01:01:53.299
I had a raise hell personality.

01:01:53.299 --> 01:01:54.447
It's only when you put
us against the wall.

01:01:54.447 --> 01:01:58.600
But a fundamentalist mentality.

01:01:58.600 --> 01:02:01.160
Once, in terms of what we
actually did and didn't do.

01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:02.190
A conservative, christian mentality.

01:02:02.190 --> 01:02:04.500
And so when I heard that you drank,

01:02:04.500 --> 01:02:06.520
when you ate the tequila worm,

01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:09.000
I was, there was a part
of me that was like,

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:10.242
is that drinking?

01:02:10.242 --> 01:02:13.130
I mean there is some tequila in that.

01:02:13.130 --> 01:02:14.740
It was definitely this vibe of,

01:02:14.740 --> 01:02:16.900
what is Link gonna do if I'm not around?

01:02:16.900 --> 01:02:18.580
Yeah, I feel like I gotta be there

01:02:18.580 --> 01:02:20.823
to keep him from falling.

01:02:22.620 --> 01:02:23.453
But I just think the worm.
And always starts.

01:02:23.453 --> 01:02:24.953
With a tequila, with a worm,

01:02:26.689 --> 01:02:28.039
the first step is the worm.

01:02:28.890 --> 01:02:32.220
Not typically, it did not taste good,

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:34.130
I mean, it probably tasted better

01:02:34.130 --> 01:02:36.290
than if it wasn't soaked
in tequila for a few months

01:02:36.290 --> 01:02:37.123
or however long that happens.

01:02:37.123 --> 01:02:41.590
I think we saw Travis Tritt
there at Walnut Creek.

01:02:41.590 --> 01:02:44.980
I went to Travis Tritt
with my dad, actually.

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:46.703
I have seen Travis Tritt in concert,

01:02:46.703 --> 01:02:48.259
I mean, I have to assume
that it was Walnut Creek.

01:02:48.259 --> 01:02:49.900
It was pretty good I remember,

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:51.890
I remember we went into
the Brooks and Dunn show

01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:54.000
and that's where I
bought the license plate

01:02:54.000 --> 01:02:55.480
that I put on the front of my truck

01:02:55.480 --> 01:02:56.940
and I bought it as a joke,

01:02:56.940 --> 01:02:58.930
I just wanna go on record and say,

01:02:58.930 --> 01:03:02.670
buying a license plate that
says Brooks and Dunn was a joke,

01:03:02.670 --> 01:03:05.280
it was irony and put it
on the front of my truck

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:07.560
but it was also like, I don't know, not.

01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:11.060
Not at the same time,
that we lived like that

01:03:11.060 --> 01:03:12.350
in a lot of ways.

01:03:12.350 --> 01:03:14.550
And they had a karaoke booth out there

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:16.870
where all the tents were,

01:03:16.870 --> 01:03:20.120
where you could get your food
and your merch and stuff,

01:03:20.120 --> 01:03:25.070
they had a karaoke booth
and I went up there and I,

01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:27.180
we both, didn't you sing too?

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:30.360
I sang Big City by Merle Haggard

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:32.670
and I remember people
would, people stopped

01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:34.520
and started watching me sing it

01:03:34.520 --> 01:03:38.120
because, oh, here's this 16
year old singing Merle Haggard,

01:03:38.120 --> 01:03:39.530
I like this guy

01:03:39.530 --> 01:03:43.430
and it really fed into
this redneck mentality.

01:03:43.430 --> 01:03:45.480
I just don't have a, I don't remember.

01:03:45.480 --> 01:03:47.140
It was fun to be a part of

01:03:48.206 --> 01:03:50.233
and I was a huge Brooks and Dunn fan,

01:03:52.860 --> 01:03:54.570
which is why we covered them.

01:03:54.570 --> 01:03:58.640
The most memorable
concert experience though,

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:03.070
I think has to be sort of the Merle,

01:04:03.070 --> 01:04:05.630
the string of Merle Haggard experiences,

01:04:05.630 --> 01:04:09.993
starting at 16 when
we'd go down to see him.

01:04:11.220 --> 01:04:13.190
At the Alabama Theater in Myrtle Beach.

01:04:13.190 --> 01:04:14.970
In Myrtle Beach and then.

01:04:14.970 --> 01:04:17.580
I had to miss a soccer game.

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:19.180
Is that, that's the night

01:04:19.180 --> 01:04:20.390
when we followed the tour bus.

01:04:20.390 --> 01:04:22.260
Yeah, I was a starter on the soccer team

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:24.740
and we drove my Nissan pickup,

01:04:24.740 --> 01:04:27.100
probably with the Brooks
and Dunn license plate

01:04:27.100 --> 01:04:30.240
on the front, all the way
down there to Myrtle beach

01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:32.630
to watch the show and then we
were just gonna drive back,

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:35.020
I think 'cause I don't
think we got a hotel room.

01:04:35.020 --> 01:04:38.680
No, of course not but he was
driving south after the show

01:04:39.660 --> 01:04:42.720
and we, these stories,

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:45.280
kind of the string of
Merle Haggard's stories

01:04:45.280 --> 01:04:48.010
are all told in detail in
the Book of Mythicality,

01:04:48.010 --> 01:04:51.120
in one of the chapters
talking about being a fan,

01:04:51.120 --> 01:04:52.043
because it kind of came full circle

01:04:52.043 --> 01:04:55.270
that we followed him as a
kid, we saw him multiple times

01:04:55.270 --> 01:04:57.883
including at Dorton Arena and then.

01:04:58.820 --> 01:05:00.950
Always the youngest guys in the crowd.

01:05:00.950 --> 01:05:03.020
It was these two teenage dudes

01:05:03.020 --> 01:05:04.810
who were not with their dads.

01:05:04.810 --> 01:05:06.150
Three decades.

01:05:06.150 --> 01:05:07.290
There were some kids who were there

01:05:07.290 --> 01:05:10.760
with grandparents or
whatever, dads but not us,

01:05:10.760 --> 01:05:14.070
we were just there completely
sincerely into this thing

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:16.600
and then of course, as an adult,

01:05:16.600 --> 01:05:21.260
having the opportunity
to meet him backstage

01:05:21.260 --> 01:05:23.400
in that weird, I'm not
gonna tell the whole story

01:05:23.400 --> 01:05:24.630
but we've told the story in detail

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:27.702
on Good Mythical Morning and
in the Book of Mythicality

01:05:27.702 --> 01:05:29.670
but then finally.

01:05:29.670 --> 01:05:31.930
Meeting him at a point
where we could see him.

01:05:31.930 --> 01:05:34.670
Getting on the tour bus with him

01:05:34.670 --> 01:05:36.440
and going to the back of the tour bus

01:05:36.440 --> 01:05:37.460
and having him kind of sit there

01:05:37.460 --> 01:05:38.963
with a towel around his neck,

01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:44.140
clearly a 70 something year old man

01:05:45.570 --> 01:05:47.980
exhausted from playing another night

01:05:47.980 --> 01:05:50.170
and these two super fans coming back there

01:05:50.170 --> 01:05:52.259
and just not knowing what to say

01:05:52.259 --> 01:05:54.080
and being that moment
where you're just like,

01:05:54.080 --> 01:05:57.150
we followed you, we
followed, when we were kids,

01:05:57.150 --> 01:06:00.590
we followed you across the state line

01:06:00.590 --> 01:06:02.850
and it's just so cool to get to meet you

01:06:02.850 --> 01:06:04.720
and you've been so, you
just don't know what to say

01:06:04.720 --> 01:06:06.332
to somebody who's been that influential

01:06:06.332 --> 01:06:07.327
and he was just kinda like,

01:06:07.327 --> 01:06:09.030
you could kind of see it on his eyes,

01:06:09.030 --> 01:06:11.180
he's like, I've heard
this a million times,

01:06:12.380 --> 01:06:14.580
all right, you got
something for me to sign?

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:17.763
Yeah, I'll sign this,
I'll sign your albums.

01:06:18.890 --> 01:06:23.000
We got the albums hanging
up in our office right there

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:23.833
that he signed,

01:06:23.833 --> 01:06:25.140
he misspelled your name
but then he corrected it.

01:06:25.140 --> 01:06:25.973
He corrected it.

01:06:25.973 --> 01:06:27.883
That was good, so I mean.

01:06:27.883 --> 01:06:29.070
It's easy to do.

01:06:29.070 --> 01:06:32.130
I'm glad that we're back
on the concert train

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:34.950
and it's about creating an experience,

01:06:34.950 --> 01:06:38.120
bringing people with you and
then having an experience

01:06:38.120 --> 01:06:40.890
with all the other people
that you don't know,

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:45.890
it's not but I think, it's
also not all fun and games

01:06:46.547 --> 01:06:48.700
when you got people
not wanting to sit down

01:06:48.700 --> 01:06:51.730
and people not wanting to
stand up, it ain't easy,

01:06:51.730 --> 01:06:53.780
there's lots of things that could happen.

01:06:53.780 --> 01:06:54.974
And I always forget about.

01:06:54.974 --> 01:06:55.807
Lots of worms to be eaten.

01:06:55.807 --> 01:06:58.360
I always forget about that element

01:06:58.360 --> 01:07:01.366
of preparing yourself for this night

01:07:01.366 --> 01:07:03.340
and then you're like, oh, well,

01:07:03.340 --> 01:07:05.160
'cause the worst is when
you're next to somebody

01:07:05.160 --> 01:07:07.883
who's just super drunk,

01:07:08.720 --> 01:07:10.190
super drunk and super loud.

01:07:10.190 --> 01:07:12.190
Spilling beer on you.

01:07:12.190 --> 01:07:13.023
And then you're just like,

01:07:13.023 --> 01:07:16.250
oh man, I can't, I'm not
gonna be able to enjoy this,

01:07:16.250 --> 01:07:18.030
it's like going to a
movie theater and it's,

01:07:18.030 --> 01:07:20.500
in a movie theater, if somebody's
being loud next to you,

01:07:20.500 --> 01:07:21.830
you can just tell them to shut up,

01:07:21.830 --> 01:07:25.320
in a concert, I'm not
gonna be the person that,

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:27.060
you don't, I'm not gonna
start a concert fight.

01:07:27.060 --> 01:07:29.150
That's why you invite more people

01:07:29.150 --> 01:07:31.630
and then you sit in the
middle of all of them,

01:07:31.630 --> 01:07:32.680
that's how you do it.

01:07:34.460 --> 01:07:36.360
It might be a good idea to get that.

01:07:38.210 --> 01:07:39.043
The Greek.

01:07:39.043 --> 01:07:40.360
I don't know about getting a season pass

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:43.780
as much as I'm just thinking,
between the Hollywood Bowl

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:46.410
and the Greek theater,
all the venues downtown

01:07:46.410 --> 01:07:48.570
where the smaller bands come, basically.

01:07:48.570 --> 01:07:49.940
That's the great thing about being in LA,

01:07:49.940 --> 01:07:51.910
you know everybody's gonna come through.

01:07:51.910 --> 01:07:54.870
Every weekend, almost
every night of the year,

01:07:54.870 --> 01:07:57.360
you could probably go and see somebody

01:07:57.360 --> 01:08:00.360
that you have some level
of familiarity with.

01:08:00.360 --> 01:08:02.223
Yeah, including Paul McCartney,

01:08:03.650 --> 01:08:06.470
he's coming to SoFi, I should
and we should go see him.

01:08:06.470 --> 01:08:08.610
I mean but the reason
why I brought that up

01:08:08.610 --> 01:08:11.563
is 'cause that's my,
I'm gonna give my rec,

01:08:13.870 --> 01:08:15.690
I gotta stay in the music zone.

01:08:15.690 --> 01:08:18.600
Another music documentary
I watched on a plane

01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:25.460
coming back home, McCartney
3, 2, 1 is a mini series,

01:08:27.640 --> 01:08:30.960
three episodes on Hulu,

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:35.090
it's just Rick Rubin and Paul McCartney

01:08:35.090 --> 01:08:38.750
at Rick Rubin's, I think
he's at Rick Rubin's studio

01:08:38.750 --> 01:08:41.150
but they're in a couple,
it's very intimate,

01:08:41.150 --> 01:08:43.410
the cameras are really
far back and zoomed in

01:08:43.410 --> 01:08:48.410
and they're just having this
very off the cuff conversation,

01:08:48.810 --> 01:08:52.463
Rick has his, if you don't
know who Rick Rubin is that's,

01:08:54.300 --> 01:08:55.700
I'd love to meet Rick Rubin,

